          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Years and International Impact</strong><br>\nStanton's influence extended internationally as she worked on issues of women's rights globally. In her later years, she focused on writing and advocating for progressive causes, including divorce reform and equal guardianship of children.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Controversy and Progressive Views</strong><br>\nStanton was often at the center of controversy for her progressive views, including her critique of organized religion’s role in the oppression of women. Her radical ideas occasionally isolated her from more conservative suffragists.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nElizabeth Cady Stanton died on October 26, 1902. She did not live to see the passage of the 19th Amendment; however, her work laid the foundation for the legal voting rights of women and the continuing fight for gender equality.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
